1.2,3],"m":"marble"},{"blob":[20.64,18.45,3.8,0.8,0.8,1.1],"m":"dark"},{"tree":[67.18,-22.21,14,4]},{"tree":[-1.65,76.09,14,4]},{"tree":[35.56,-46.79,14,4],"shape":"poplar"},{"tree":[-35.56,54.79,14,4],"shape":"poplar"},{"tree":[9.84,-62.36,14,4]},{"tree":[-60.63,34.79,14,4]},{"tree":[-24.98,-56.22,14,4]},{"tree":[-65.13,1.12,14,4]},{"tree":[-49.15,-30.41,14,4]}],"notes":"George Applegarth's Legion of Honor (1924) in Lincoln Park, a three-quarter-scale version of the Palais de la Légion d'Honneur (Hôtel de Salm) in Paris: the Court of Honour entered through a triumphal arch in the front screen, Ionic colonnades down its sides, the main block at the back with its six-column Corinthian portico, a balustraded cornice and the rotunda's low dome on the far side; I. M. Pei-era glass pyramid skylight (1995 expansion) in the court and Rodin's Thinker before the arch. The plan stands about 55° off north, as in the OpenStreetMap ground, so every part is rasterised into rows of boxes along its real bearing (the court opening to the southeast is a reading of the ground; check it). Sizes from the ground footprint (about 100 by 80 m); heights, column counts and the dome's size guessed from general knowledge. No photographs were available."}